# 30 — Migrations: write the file, stop; never apply

Schema truth is `supabase/migrations/` (append-only). You WRITE migration files and end the part
with a MIGRATION stop. You never apply a migration — not on ethio-staging, not on ethio-prod, not
through an MCP server, the `supabase` CLI, `psql`, a SQL editor or any other path. The supervisor
reviews the file and applies it on ethio-staging (where its proofs run on a real schema) and reads
back its mark; production is applied behind the operator's own approval. Then the supervisor sends
`continue`.

## Order inside a turn

A migration comes as early as its part allows — the code that depends on the schema follows it. A
turn that lands a door and its screens writes the door migration first, STOPs, and builds the screens
after `continue`. Never an empty or placeholder migration.

## Content laws

- File name: the Supabase stamp form `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_<uuid>.sql` (UTC now, a fresh uuid). One
  concern per file; a very large migration (about 25 KB or more) is split by concern into ordered
  parts, each with its own mark, proofs and stop.
- APPEND-ONLY: corrections are new migrations; a landed file is never edited; destructive changes
  only when the brief says so in words.
- BORN CLOSED: `CREATE TABLE` → `REVOKE ALL ON <table> FROM PUBLIC, anon, authenticated;` →
  `GRANT ALL ON <table> TO service_role;` → `ALTER TABLE … ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;` →
  per-operation policies (a service-only table: four deny policies — SELECT `USING (false)`, INSERT
  `WITH CHECK (false)`, UPDATE, DELETE). A grant to anon, authenticated or PUBLIC needs its line in
  `scripts/public-surface-allowlist.txt` (`object | reason`). Every new sequence and function is born
  closed; every new function restates `REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION … FROM PUBLIC, anon;` and its GRANTs
  (`GRANT EXECUTE … TO authenticated` where the app calls it; `GRANT ALL … TO service_role`).
  `SECURITY DEFINER` functions carry `SET search_path = public`. Client reads only via gated
  SECURITY DEFINER RPCs; `rpc()` argument and column names are copied from the SQL declaration.
- PERSONAL DATA: a column naming a person references `public.profiles(user_id)`, never `auth.users`
  (only `profiles` and `user_roles` may); personal tables carry `home_country_code`; every table has
  `created_at` / `updated_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()` with the
  `public.update_updated_at_column()` trigger; money is never a float; a table with a person column
  gets its RESET or EXEMPT line in `e2e/helpers/pool-reset-map.ts`.
- FUNCTIONS: never patch a body by text anchor. Redeclare WHOLE from the LIVE definition the brief
  hands you (`pg_get_functiondef` with `provolatile`, `prosecdef`, `proconfig` as read on ethio-prod):
  the new body is byte for byte the old one apart from the named change; `CREATE OR REPLACE`, no DROP
  unless the argument list or return shape changes (then DROP + CREATE, restating the dropped
  function's REVOKE and GRANTs); a body over about 10 KB is redeclared by computed insertion, guarded
  by md5. A nullable argument becomes optional in the generated types only when it is trailing with
  `DEFAULT NULL` — design signatures so. No app-reachable function issues a DELETE or UPDATE without
  a WHERE clause.
- STATE: listing status changes only inside its state machine; writer order in every mutating RPC is
  gates (permission → step-up → scope) → capture (old → new) → mutate; a refused attempt leaves no
  trace. Totality gates define the empty set explicitly.
- MARK: every migration inserts its own mark —
  `INSERT INTO public.migration_marks (version) VALUES ('<MARK>') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;` — where
  `<MARK>` is a 14-digit UTC stamp later than the file's own stamp and above every mark in the
  ledger (the brief gives the floor). A wrong mark is healed by a later migration's UPDATE, never by
  editing a landed file. A `-- e2e-areas:` header line names the spec areas the change selects (no
  line → all).
- PROOFS: one `DO $$ … $$` block that creates its own scratch rows (`e2e_mig_` tables, `e2e-` rows)
  and removes them, runs as a scratch user or with no caller, never reads a real account or place,
  checks privileges with `has_table_privilege` / `has_function_privilege`, and `RAISE EXCEPTION`s on
  any failure — a clean apply is the pass; a proof that skips with a NOTICE is a weakened proof. A
  statement that spans a whole table is proven through a service-only core that takes an id scope
  (`<name>_core(p_only uuid[])`) called with the proof's own scratch ids; the live wrapper is never
  called by the proof. A call and the check of its effect are separate statements.
- A proof keys its scratch rows on its own scratch key (`gen_random_uuid()`, an `e2e-` value),
  never on the current second (`epoch FROM now()` / `clock_timestamp()`) or a fixed
  `00000000-` uuid (CF-PROOF-KEY-1, `scripts/conformance/migration-proof-keys.test.ts`).
- A proof that judges a value at a window edge reads the clock just before the call, as the code
  under test does, and keeps a margin of one tick: a clock read once at the top of the block and
  compared with a function that reads its own failed one start in a hundred (INC-1028, DEC-316). List RPCs ship
  their index and an EXPLAIN in the proof. A function a route calls is also proven by a route test.
- CLOSERS in-file, last: the REVOKE / GRANT lines, then the mark.
- Run `bash scripts/check-migrations.sh` and paste its guard lines ("Self-marking guard OK" among
  them) before you commit.

## The MIGRATION stop report (then push and stop)

For each file: its name; `sha256sum`; the declared mark; the objects it creates or redeclares; the
proofs it carries; the guard lines; and the apply pairing line, exactly:
`apply <uuid-fragment of the file name> → expect mark <MARK>`. Write it as
`docs/governance/reports/<brief-stem>.stop-<n>.md` — first line `MIGRATION — <stem> part X:
<subject>`, then DONE / LEFT / CI / FILES, the per-file lines and the pairing line — commit it on its
own, push the branch, open or update the draft pull request, say so in the chat and stop. The pull
request's CI builds its temporary backends from every migration in the repository, so your file's
proofs run there; its preflight job reports "STAGING BEHIND" until ethio-staging holds the mark —
expected, and named in the report.
